<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Louvain</h1><p class="st-meta">1003 CE – 1183 CE (approx.) · capital Leuven</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The county around Leuven and Brussels from which the Reginarid counts built the Duchy of Brabant.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1003 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambert-i-count-of-louvain">Lambert I the Bearded</a>, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginarids">Reginarid</a> family, took the title of count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>. He held lands around Leuven and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, which was growing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senne">Senne</a>, and expanded them through war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a> and neighbouring lords.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a fertile, densely settled region on the trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> grew as market towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-i-of-louvain">Godfrey I of Louvain</a> was named duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a> in 1106, giving the family the ducal title and a claim to authority over the whole region, and they also styled themselves landgraves of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1183 Count Henry I began using the title duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-schwabisch-hall">Diet of Schwäbisch Hall</a> in 1190 the county became the core of the Duchy of Brabant, whose dukes ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-louvain.jpg" alt="County of Louvain" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armoiries_de_Vianden_3.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1003 CE – 1183 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leuven</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lambert I of Louvain, Godfrey I of Louvain, Henry I of Brabant</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Leuven, Landgraviate of Brabant</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1093&amp;focus=county-of-louvain" aria-label="County of Louvain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4787 413 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Louvain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5107 669L5229 694M5107 669L5098 671"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5098 671h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5107 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5107 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Leuven. <a href="/atlas?year=1093&amp;focus=county-of-louvain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
